    \field{sortinit}{R}
    \field{sortinithash}{R}
    \field{abstract}{%
    People tweet more than 100 Million times daily, yielding a noisy, informal,
  but sometimes informative corpus of 140-character messages that mirrors the
  zeitgeist in an unprecedented manner. The performance of standard NLP tools
  is severely degraded on tweets. This paper addresses this issue by
  re-building the NLP pipeline beginning with part-of-speech tagging, through
  chunking, to named-entity recognition. Our novel T-ner system doubles F1
  score compared with the Stanford NER system. T-ner leverages the redundancy
  inherent in tweets to achieve this performance, using LabeledLDA to exploit
  Freebase dictionaries as a source of distant supervision. LabeledLDA
  outperforms co-training, increasing F1 by 25% over ten common entity
  types.Our NLP tools are available at: http://github.com/aritter/twitter_nlp%
    }
